INT. STORAGE UNIT 214 - DAY

HANDHELD. The camera wobbles down a corridor of orange roll-up doors. Fluorescent hum. Priya Nair, 30s, holds the lens on herself, then swings it to a padlocked unit.

PRIYA
(to camera)
Day three of the interviews. Everyone says Mr. Okafor's unit is just old furniture.

An old man in a faded field jacket stands by the door, keys in hand. Not hostile. Not welcoming either.

ON CAMERA: the old man rubs his thumb over the padlock.

OLD MAN
Furniture. Yes. My wife's chairs.

PRIYA
Can you say your name for the record?

OLD MAN
Is it recording now?

The phone in Priya's pocket BUZZES. She checks it, winces.

PRIYA
Sorry. My producer.

She thumbs the speaker on. Her phone crackles.

TEO (V.O.)
(tinny)
Are you inside yet? Legal wants the release signed before you open anything.

PRIYA
He signed it. We're fine.

TEO (V.O.)
Priya, listen. The records came back. That name, Okafor, it's not --

The call DROPS. Priya stares at the screen. Three bars. Nothing.

The old man slides the key in. The lock CLACKS open. The door RATTLES upward on its track.

Inside: two chairs. A lamp. And a dozen olive-green crates stacked to the ceiling, each stencilled with a unit patch.

OLD MAN (CONT'D)
My name is Walt Okafor. I was never in the unit I told you about.

Behind them, THE MOVERS, three men in grey coveralls, wheel a dolly down the corridor and stop dead.

THE MOVERS
(together)
Mr. Okafor?

WALT
Not yet, boys.

PRIYA
Walt, what is in those crates?

WALT
Open one.

The camera drops. We hear only her breath, a lid CREAKING, and then silence.

WALT (CONT'D)
They are not mine. I only kept them.

PRIYA
(whispering)
These are names.

WALT
Four hundred and six.

PRIYA
Walt, stop.
WALT ^
I'm done lying.

The corridor lights FLICKER and go out.
